Thought: Start with mass spectrometry to obtain the molecular weight and possibly the molecular formula.
Action: mass_spectrometry_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: The most intense peak in the mass spectrometry data is at m/z 235.1395, which likely corresponds to the molecular weight of the compound. To determine the molecular formula, I will use this information to find possible isomers.
Action: obtain_isomers_from_molecular_formula
Action Input: {"molecular_formula": "C15H17NO"}

Thought: I have obtained several possible isomers for the molecular formula C15H17NO. To narrow down the correct structure, I will use the proton NMR spectra to compare the chemical shifts with the expected shifts for these isomers.
Action: proton_nmr_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: Since the proton NMR prediction failed, I will use the carbon NMR spectra to help identify the correct isomer by comparing the carbon chemical shifts with expected values for the isomers.
Action: carbon_nmr_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: Both proton and carbon NMR predictions have failed. I will now use the IR spectra to identify functional groups, which might help in determining the correct isomer.
Action: ir_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: The IR spectra show a peak at 3357 cm-1, which is indicative of an N-H stretch, suggesting the presence of an amine or amide group. This information can help narrow down the isomers. I will now validate the SMILES strings of the isomers to ensure they are correct and then select the one that matches the IR data.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "C=C=CC1=CC=C(C=C1)CC(=O)N2CCCC2"}

Thought: The SMILES string "C=C=CC1=CC=C(C=C1)CC(=O)N2CCCC2" is valid. This structure contains an amide group, which is consistent with the IR peak at 3357 cm-1. I will now validate the remaining SMILES strings to ensure they are correct and compare them to the IR data.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "CC(C)(C)C1=NC=C(C=C1)OC2=CC=CC=C2"}

Thought: The SMILES string "CC(C)(C)C1=NC=C(C=C1)OC2=CC=CC=C2" is valid, but it contains an ether and a pyridine ring, which do not match the IR data indicating an N-H stretch. I will continue validating the remaining SMILES strings.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "[HH].CC1=CC=C(C=C1)C2=CC=C(C=C2)C(=O)NC"}

Thought: The SMILES string "[HH].CC1=CC=C(C=C1)C2=CC=C(C=C2)C(=O)NC" is valid and contains an amide group, which matches the IR data indicating an N-H stretch. I will validate the remaining SMILES strings to ensure completeness.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "CC1=CC=CC=C1CCOC2=CC=C(C=C2)N"}

Thought: The SMILES string "CC1=CC=CC=C1CCOC2=CC=C(C=C2)N" is valid, but it contains an ether and an amine, which do not fully match the IR data indicating an N-H stretch typical of an amide. I will validate the last SMILES string.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "C[C@]12[C@H](C3CCC1C3=O)N(C4=CC=CC=C24)C"}
